**Theorem 30.1 (unrestricted original lower versus restricted-menu
uppers).** For every $D\ge3$ and $1\le k\le2^D-1$,

$$
m(D)\le H_D(k)\le\min\{M_1(D),m(D)^k\},
\tag{GDB.118}
$$

and

$$
\log_2M_1(D)-\log_2H_D(k)
\ge\max\left\{0,\ 2^{D-3}\log_2R_{\rm a}-k\log_2m(D)\right\}.
\tag{GDB.119}
$$

Proof. The singleton $\{R^D\}$ is among the menus in $H_D(k)$,
so Theorem 28.1 gives the first lower inequality. Every scheme for
the full original menu serves each restricted menu without changing
its preparation or code. Thus $M_D(Q)\le M_1(D)$, and maximization
gives the first upper. Theorem 29.1 and monotonicity of $m$ give
$M_D(Q)\le m(D)^{|Q|}\le m(D)^k$, proving the other upper.

Now reuse [NCE, Theorem 17.1][NCE], with its full original
all-competitor quantifier:

$$
M_1(D)\ge\left\lceil R_{\rm a}^{\,2^{D-3}}\right\rceil.
\tag{GDB.120}
$$

It applies to every original export, retaining arbitrary correlated
fibers and all finite queries; no new local restriction is inserted.
Subtract $\log_2H_D(k)\le k\log_2m(D)$ from its logarithmic lower.
The difference is also nonnegative by $H_D(k)\le M_1(D)$, giving
(GDB.119). This uses the credited NCE converse and proves a new
comparison with the restricted-menu optima, rather than a new converse
endpoint. $\square$

